"The Distribution of Sandpile Groups of Random Graphs with their Pairings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.07078)\nciteturn2academia11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Authors: Eliot Hodges ... We determine the distribution of the sandpile group (also known as the Jacobian) of the Erd\\H{o}s-R\\'{e}nyi random graph $G(n,q)$ along with its canonical duality pairing as $n$ tends to infinity, fully resolving a conjecture from 2015 due to Clancy, Leake, and Payne and generalizing the result by Wood on the groups.\nTitle: The Distribution of Sandpile Groups of Random Graphs with their Pairings\nAuthors: Eliot Hodges\nDate: Mon Nov 13 05:02:42 2023\n\nWe determine the distribution of the sandpile group (also known as the Jacobian) of the Erd\\H{o}s-R\\'{e}nyi random graph $G(n,q)$ along with its canonical duality pairing as $n$ tends to infinity, fully resolving a conjecture from 2015 due to Clancy, Leake, and Payne and generalizing the result by Wood on the groups. In particular, we show that a finite abelian $p$-group $G$ equipped with a perfect symmetric pairing $\\delta$ appears as the Sylow $p$-part of the sandpile group and its pairing with frequency inversely proportional to $|G||\\mathrm{Aut}(G,\\delta)|$, where $\\mathrm{Aut}(G,\\delta)$ is the set of automorphisms of $G$ preserving the pairing $\\delta$. While this distribution is related to the Cohen-Lenstra distribution, the two distributions are not the same on account of the additional algebraic data of the pairing. The proof utilizes the moment method: we first compute a complete set of moments for our random variable (the average number of epimorphisms from our random object to a fixed object in the category of interest) and then show the moments determine the distribution. To obtain the moments, we prove a universality result for the moments of cokernels of random symmetric integral matrices whose dual groups are equipped with symmetric pairings that is strong enough to handle both the dependence in the diagonal entries and the additional data of the pairing. We then apply results due to Sawin and Wood to show that these moments determine a unique distribution.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistribution of sandpile groups of directed and undirected bipartite graphs (https://escholarship.org/content/qt3f60f3nf/qt3f60f3nf_noSplash_32e3a2da227ebbab7ccb7f22f8f9f093.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Hodges [11] proved the corresponding result for the distribution of the cokernel along with its pairing. ... ## 1.1.3 Sandpile groups of random graphs\n**Theorem 1.1.8.** [18] Let \\(X_n\\in \\mathrm{M}_n(\\mathbb Z)\\) be a random symmetric matrix, whose entries on and below the diagonal are independent, identically distributed and \\(\\epsilon\\)-balanced at every prime \\(p\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbb P[\\operatorname{Coker}(X_n)\\text{ is cyclic}]\n=\n\\prod_{i=1}^{\\infty}\\zeta(2i+1)^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nHodges [11] proved the corresponding result for the distribution of the cokernel along with its pairing.\n\n**Theorem 1.1.9.** [11] Fix a prime \\(p\\). Let \\(X_n\\in \\mathrm{M}_n(\\mathbb Z_p)\\) be a random symmetric matrix, whose entries on and below the diagonal are independent and \\(\\epsilon\\)-balanced. Let \\(G\\) be a finite abelian \\(p\\)-group equipped with a perfect symmetric bilinear pairing \\(\\delta\\). Then\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\n\\mathbb P[(\\operatorname{Coker}(X_n),\\delta_{X_n})\\cong(G,\\delta)]\n=\n\\frac{1}{|G||\\operatorname{Aut}(G,\\delta)|}\n\\prod_{k\\geq 0}(1-p^{-1-2k}).\n\\]\n\nShen [20] later reproved this symmetric universality theorem by a different method and obtained effective error bounds.\n\n## 1.1.3 Sandpile groups of random graphs\n\nLet \\(\\Gamma\\) be a graph on vertices \\(1,\\ldots,n\\) without loops. Write \\(\\deg(i,j)\\) for the number of edges between \\(i\\) and \\(j\\), and\n\n\\[\n\\deg(j)=\\sum_{i:i\\neq j}\\deg(i,j).\n\\]\n\nDefine the Laplacian \\(L(\\Gamma)\\) by\n\n\\[\nL_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\deg(i,j), & i\\neq j,\\\\\n-\\deg(j), & i=j.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nso that\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}L_{ij}=0\n\\]\n\nfor all \\(j\\). Let \\(\\mathbb Z_0^n\\) be the subgroup of \\(\\mathbb Z^n\\) consisting of vectors whose coordinates sum to 0. Then\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}(L)\\subseteq\\mathbb Z_0^n,\n\\]\n\nand the sandpile group of \\(\\Gamma\\) is\n\n\\[\nS_\\Gamma:=\\mathbb Z_0^n/\\operatorname{Im}(L).\n\\]\n\n6--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe distribution of sandpile groups of random graphs with their pairings - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/The_distribution_of_sandpile_groups_of_random_graphs_with_their_pairings)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Authors Eliot Hodges ...   * Grothendieck's pairing on component groups of Jacobians. ... This page was built for publication: The distribution of sandpile groups of random graphs with their pairings\n# The distribution of sandpile groups of random graphs with their pairings\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1090/TRAN/9244MaRDI QIDQ6631317zbMATH OpenFDO\n\nAuthors Eliot Hodges\n\nPublication date 1 November 2024\n\nPublished in Transactions of the American Mathematical Society (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://doi.org/10.1090/TRAN/9244\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nGraphs and linear algebra (matrices, eigenvalues, etc.) (05C50) Random graphs (graph-theoretic aspects) (05C80) Random matrices (algebraic aspects) (15B52) Random matrices (probabilistic aspects) (60B20)\n\nRecommendations\n\n  * The distribution of sandpile groups of random graphs\n  * The distribution of sandpile groups of random regular graphs\n  * Sandpile groups of random bipartite graphs\n  * The rank of the sandpile group of random directed bip