property.\nThe argument includes a computer-aided classiﬁcation of the minor minimal\nnot 2-apex graphs through order 9 and size 23.\nIntroduction\nA graph is n-apex if deleting n or fewer vertices results in a planar graph.\nKuratowski [K] showed the 0-apex or planar graphs are characterized by the ob-\nstruction set O0 = {K5, K3,3}. Using the formulation of Wagner [W], a graph is\nplanar if and only if it has neither K5 nor K3,3 as a minor. Recall that H is a minor\nof the graph G if H can be obtained by contracting edges in a subgraph of G. As\nthe n-apex property is minor closed, it follows from the Graph Minor Theorem of\nRobertson and Seymour [RS] that there is a corresponding ﬁnite obstruction set\nOn. For each n, On is the set of graphs that are not n-apex even though every\nproper minor is. We say that the elements of On are minor minimal not n-apex\nor MMNnA.\nDetermining this obstruction set is likely to be quite diﬃcult. It’s known that,\nalready, |O1| > 150 [P], and we expect that |On| grows quickly in size with n. In\nthe current paper we report on computer searches that show |O2| ≥82, |O3| ≥601,\n|O4| ≥520, and |O5| ≥608.\nFigure 1. ∇Y and Y∇moves.\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary 05C10.\nc\n⃝2017 American Mathematical Society\n137--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Hodges_E in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AHodges_E+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; @misc{2311.07078, author = {Eliot Hodges}, title = {{T}he {D}istribution of {S}andpile {G}roups of {R}andom {G}raphs with their {P}airings}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2311.07078}, note = {arXiv:2311.07078v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We determine the distribution of the sandpile group (also known as the Jacobian) of the Erdős-Rényi random graph $G(n,q)$ along with its canonical duality pairing as $n$ tends to infinity, fully resolving a conjecture from 2015 due to Clancy, Leake, and Payne and generalizing the result by Wood on the groups.\n  * The Distribution of Sandpile Groups of Random Graphs with their Pairings\n\nEliot Hodges\n\nNov 14 2023 math.CO math.NT math.PR arXiv:2311.07078v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2311.07078, author = {Eliot Hodges}, title = {{T}he {D}istribution of {S}andpile {G}roups of {R}andom {G}raphs with their {P}airings}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2311.07078}, note = {arXiv:2311.07078v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe determine the distribution of the sandpile group (also known as the Jacobian) of the Erdős-Rényi random graph $G(n,q)$ along with its canonical duality pairing as $n$ tends to infinity, fully resolving a conjecture from 2015 due to Clancy, Leake, and Payne and generalizing the result by Wood on the groups. In particular, we show that a finite abelian $p$-group $G$ equipped with a perfect symmetric pairing $\\delta$ appears as the Sylow $p$-part of the sandpile group and its pairing with frequency inversely proportional to $|G||\\mathrm{Aut}(G,\\delta)|$, where $\\mathrm{Aut}(G,\\delta)$ is the set of automorphisms of $G$ preserving the pairing $\\delta$. While this distribution is related to the Cohen-Lenstra distribution, the two distributions are not the same on account of the additional algebraic data of the pairing. The proof utilizes the moment method: we first compute a complete set of moments for our random variable (the average number of epimorphisms from our random object to a fixed object in the category of interest) and then show the moments determine the distribution. To obtain the moments, we prove a universality result for the moments of cokernels of random symmetric integral matrices whose dual groups are equipped with symmetric pairings that is strong enough to handle both the dependence in the diagonal entries and the additional data of the pairing. We then apply results due to Sawin and Wood to show that these moments determine a unique distribution.\n\n  * On Promotion and Quasi-tangled Labelings of Posets\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe 𝐾_{𝑛+5} and 𝐾_{3²,1ⁿ} families and obstructions to 𝑛-apex. (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/317074007_The_5_and_31_families_and_obstructions_to_-apex)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; To read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors. ... We give evidence in support of a conjecture that the Y∇Y families of Kn+5 and K3²,1n are minor minimal obstructions for the n-apex property. ... A landmark problem that attracted particular attention (see e.g., [63,65,89]) is the one of identifying {K 5 , K 3,3 } (1) , i.e., characterizing 1-apex planar graphs.\n\n# The 𝐾_{𝑛+5} and 𝐾_{3²,1ⁿ} families and obstructions to 𝑛-apex.\n\n  * January 2017\n\nDOI:10.1090/conm/689/13856\n\n  * In book: Knots, Links, Spatial Graphs, and Algebraic Invariants (pp.137-158)\n\nAuthors:\n\nThomas W. Mattman\n\n  * California State University, Chico\n\nMichael Pierce\n\nMichael Pierce\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe give evidence in support of a conjecture that the Y∇Y families of Kn+5 and K3²,1n are minor minimal obstructions for the n-apex property. The argument includes a computer-aided classification of the minor minimal not 2-apex graphs through order 9 and size 23.\n\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (7)\n\n## References (9)\n\n... To our knowledge, no result is known able to exactly identify obs(G i ), for all i ∈ N. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHÈSE POUR OBTENIR LE GRADE DE DOCTEUR (https://www.irif.fr/~stamoulis/2023_STAMOULIS_thesis_final.pdf)\nciteturn